Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.210 align:start position:0%
 
alright guys so earlier this week I was  at the premiere here in Hollywood Los  Angeles for the no safe spaces  documentary this is a movie that's been  about two and a half years in the works  by two buddies of mine Dennis Prager  who's obviously been on this show many  times and I've been on his radio show a  gajillion times and done some videos for  Prager you and Adam Carolla who's also  been on this show and I've been on his  show a bunch of times  the premise of the movie all started  about three or four years ago when as I  know you guys are well aware a lot of  this campus craziness started and I'm  not just talking about protests but all  the things that you guys know about I'm  talking about riots and attacking police  officers and destroying public property  and stopping speakers from coming to  campus or literally locking speakers in  rooms or attacking professors or deep  platforming people or a series of the  other things that I've been talking  about on this show for a while so the  movie after about two years in the works  finally debuted here in Los Angeles  I'll pimp it out again later but you can  go to no safe spaces dot-com to find out  where the the movies premiering in your  city or hopefully close by you guys  they're still working on getting wide  release but in the first theater that  they premiered in it already had that I  think the second highest gross returns  for a documentary of all time after  Michael Moore is sicko anyway so I saw  it this week and there were a bunch of  people at the at the red carpet premiere  a lot of my crew was there from ben  shapiro and larry elder and and just a  series a bunch of guys Andrew Clayman  was there and Kurt Schlichter and a  whole bunch of people but the movie  itself and this is why I'm doing the  video the movie was spectacular it  really was like watching sort of all of  the things that I've been talking about  in this room for the last couple years  sort of all kind of pieced together so  the movie talks about the Brett  Weinstein Heather huiying debacle  basically at Evergreen State that I know  you guys are well aware about where they  basically protested a day where white  people were not  he allowed would not be allowed to come  to campus which was a flip on a  tradition that they had at Evergreen for  many years where one day a year black  students would not come to campus to  protest what they believed to be  perceived racism or racism of the past  Bret never had a problem with that  heather never had a problem with that in  the past but this year to tell students  that they couldn't come to school  because of the color of their skin they  did feel that was racist and of course  it is and they basically got chased off  campus Brett and Heather both lost there  in effect what were tenured positions  and they had to settle with the school  for $500,000 which sounds like a lot of  money right everyone would love 500  grand but that's for two professional  people with children who had tenured  positions at at a school so that talks  about that it talks about the Jordan  Peterson story fighting bill c-17 in  Canada about the trans pronouns he talks  about Lindsay Shepard who's been on the  show who was the student at Wilfrid  Laurier University who all she did was  in a class show a video of Jordan  Peterson and she was taken in by the  diversity crew or whatever you want to  call the diversity department at the  University and basically lambasted and  how she and Jordan ended up in lawsuits  with the university they talked about  the six hundred thousand dollars that it  took to secure Berkeley University so  that the very scary Ben Shapiro could  speak there and they they go on the  ground and they show you footage on the  ground and they do some fun animated  things and and a whole bunch of other  stuff but it was really interesting to  me because you know in the now it's a  little bit over four years that I've  been doing this this version of the  Reuben report it was really in September  of 2015 when I started doing this show  as a long-form interview show and I'm  sure many of you guys remember that  original interview with Sam Harris and  it was just like wow so many of the of  the people that I have talked to in this  room have been going through this and  and you know I've been through versions  of this about three years ago I spoke at  UCLA with Miley innopolis it feels like  a lifetime ago already and you know  students outside were we're throwing  things and they created a human chain  so none of us could get in they created  what was a human wall in effect so I  guess they're not really against walls  they just don't like Trump's wall but  you know they were pulling fire alarms  and there were bomb threats and students  screaming and all of these things and it  was interesting because Milo even said  to me in the midst of the whole thing  he's like you know if they would if the  protesters really weren't here I guess I  wouldn't be here either but there's so  many interesting things Brett makes a  really great point about this at the at  the end of the movie and I don't want to  give away too much because I really do  hope you guys will see it cuz it felt  important it was it was important and  edgy and real and it's also funny at  times but Brett makes a really  interesting point that you know it seems  like oh it's like these academics and  the YouTube crew they're so focused on  the college students and why are you  focusing on 18 19 21 year olds and it's  like there's actually a reason for that  and the reason for that is that these  kids that are in school now and they are  kids in effect these are kids who go to  a school to learn from adults right  they've sort of removed all the adults  from the equation we're now  administrators and college staff and  professors are cowed into silence and  there's many examples that they show  this in the movie and the problem is  that in the place where students are  supposed to learn the most that is  supposed to be the reverse of a safe  space right you're supposed to go to  college you have a sort of limited set  of ideas that you've learned as a young  person and that you've learned through  high school and whatever your family has  taught you whatever your ethnic  background is or religious beliefs or or  a religious beliefs whatever they are  you go to college and then you're  supposed to learn a wide variety of  things you're supposed to be hit with  new ideas you're supposed to be smacked  with things that will challenge you and  all those things and what's happened is  as we've removed that from the equation  and we've said to people you're not  going to be offended here you're not  going to be challenged here all of those  things what do you think you're really  doing I mean for the people this is a  perfect example of the phrase that I say  all too often which is that the road to  hell is paved with good intentions right  like so I believe that the people who  set up safe spaces the people who  instigate trigger warnings meaning that  if you walk into a class there will  actually be a sign outside of the class  and the sign will say you might hear  something about  violence or about gender or some other  scary topic you might hear some version  of these things and that you should just  watch out before you sit down but the  more that we do this book the more that  we give these kids spaces where they'll  never be challenged the more that we  warned them about potentially things  that might offend them what do you think  you're doing to these kids you're  actually whacking them in the knees  you're hitting them in the knees with a  baseball bat before you send them out to  the world because then they get out to  the world and the world is a messed up  scary place where you have to fight for  yours and you have to be ready to engage  with people who are different than you  and all of those things so I just really  felt like the movie was such a beautiful  mosaic of the things not only that I  care about and talk about but of so many  of the people who I love and admire who  have been here that I've had important  conversations with and I hope you guys  will check it out and you know I can  tell you as someone even now that you  know I speak at college campuses  probably four or five times a month I'm  actually as we take this right now I'm  heading out to Sacramento State tonight  it's like I find most of the students  that come to my events now I get it it's  a selected group of people right because  if you basically like me you're sort of  relatively open-minded you're willing to  talk about you know the differences that  we may have on important issues like  abortion or on taxes or whatever it  might be and you're willing to agree to  disagree  but I find just in the last couple weeks  I did High Point University I did  Liberty University and what I found is  that most students they don't want to be  treated like children now again it's a  self-selected group that comes to my  thing so maybe the students that are  outside of that they really do but it's  really not for the students to decide we  need more adults in the room and that's  what the movies about one of the  scariest things that they point out in  the movie is that right now a polls show  I think it was a Pew poll that they  referenced that over 50% of college  students believe that free speech  shouldn't include hate speech which the  statement itself doesn't even make sense  because a the Supreme Court has said  there's no such thing as hate speech we  have very limited protections around  speech you can't yell fire in a crowded  theater you can't incite to violence but  hate speech anything could be defined as  hate speech hate speech is an amorphous  term that anyone can use to say anything  about anybody so it'll be like someone  to be like oh you will you can't use  hate speech but you're a Nazi well it's  in that hate speech I mean we create  this really we  system of pressures that that doesn't  really make sense but the idea that  young people think that there that a  there should be hate speech laws in that  free speech that the First Amendment in  effect shouldn't protect against hate  speech that's exactly what the First  Amendment is for we've got the document  we've got the number one amendment in  the Bill of Rights that enshrines our  ability to battle it all out so that  we're not killing each other on the  streets and that's what we've done here  in America better than anywhere else so  if any of this makes any sense to you  check out the movie I normally don't do  a promo like this I'm also in it I'm  pretty I'm pretty good if I do say so  myself but there's there's liberals in  it there's conservatives in it there's  libertarians there's academics there's  comedians it's really really great check  out no safe spaces calm if you're  looking for more honest and thoughtful  conversations about free speech instead  of non-stop yelling check out our free  speech playlist and if you want to watch  full interviews on a variety of topics  check out our full episode playlists  there all right over here and to get  notified of all future videos be sure to  subscribe and click the notification  bell

